usability
  the S4 validity prefix ("invalid class ... object:") leaks internal
    class names into user-facing validation errors; stripping it
    consistently is a package-wide cosmetic pass.

performance (measure before acting)
  rbart_vi custom-prior R loop pays a full bridge round trip per
    posterior sample (rbart.R: setOffset + run(0L, 1L), ~10 fresh
    SEXPs per call in bartcore_run); built-in priors already bypass
    via in-core. Profile before touching; only that path pays.
  test-fit routing is serial over test rows (chain.hpp findBottom per
    row per tree); rows are independent chains so parallelizing is
    order-preserving - only matters when numTest ~ n.
  x86 SIMD is silently scalar: the SSE2/AVX bodies of
    misc_addVectorsInPlace, misc_subtractVectorsInPlace, and
    misc_setVectorToConstant contain scalar loops with the real
    intrinsics commented out (linearAlgebra_sse2.c:173-215 and
    siblings); dispatch tables and cpuid detection all wired; NEON
    arms live. Likely LOW impact - the scalar bodies autovectorize at
    -O2 - so either restore the intrinsics after measuring on x86 or
    simplify away the dead dispatch weight.
    misc_setIndexedVectorToConstant never entered the dispatch table.
    The SSR kernel has no SIMD leaf, unlike its mean/variance neighbors.
  within-chain threading: ASSESSED AND DEFERRED. Backfitting serializes
    trees, so parallelism lives inside each O(n) pass, costing ~3
    barrier syncs x numTrees per sweep (~300 at the defaults) against
    2.1 ms/iteration at n = 10^4 - overhead on the order of the win.
    Headroom exists only at n >= 10^5 (DRAM-bound, 31 ms/iteration),
    and wiring it makes results depend on n.threads. Revisit if
    single-chain n ~ 10^5 workloads materialize; the dormant htm
    machinery is the starting point.
  linear-leaf score->draw crossproduct reuse: needs a persistent
    per-node stats cache the design deliberately avoids ("move
    rollbacks need no cache invalidation", model.hpp:162-165); not the
    free win the old note implied.

tests
  grouped rbart_vi fits stay out of the equivalence gate (their
    multi-chain result shape does not fit the single-fit summary); add
    a scenario only if a grouped refactor is planned.
  post-mutation assertions are near-tautologies (is.finite only) in
    the older linear/gp mutation tests; mutate-then-serialize covers
    the bitwise side, but a from-scratch comparison after mutation
    would strengthen the rest. It must be statistical, not identical:
    setPredictor re-quantizes differently from a fresh build (see the
    MIA setPredictor test).

